% ===========================================================================
% EXTRA PUBLICATIONS -- BibTeX for anything that is NOT in your ORCID record.
%
% Paste entries below. They are merged into the Publications page by the same
% generator that reads ORCID, sorted into the correct year, displayed in
% exactly the same style, and announced on the News page as well.
%
% HOW TO USE
%   1. Paste a normal BibTeX entry below (from the publisher, from Zotero, or
%      from Google Scholar's "Cite > BibTeX").
%   2. Run:   make update
%   3. Commit both www/publications-extra.bib and the regenerated
%      www/publications.jemdoc (and www/news.jemdoc).
%
% RULES
%   * Lines starting with % are comments. Keep an entry commented out until you
%     are ready to publish it.
%   * The order in this file does not matter: the page is always sorted by year,
%     newest first.
%   * An entry whose DOI or title already appears in ORCID is skipped, so it is
%     safe to paste something today and let ORCID pick it up later.
%   * To remove an entry from the site, delete it here and re-run.
%   * `make update` needs the internet. To rebuild the generated pages from this
%     file alone (no ORCID, no Crossref), run:
%         python tools/update_site.py --offline
%
% WHAT THE GENERATOR READS
%   @article                                    -> Journal
%   @inproceedings, @conference                 -> Conference
%   @incollection, @inbook, @book               -> Book chapter
%   @phdthesis, @mastersthesis                  -> Thesis
%   @techreport                                 -> Report
%   @unpublished, @misc                         -> Preprint / Other
%
%   author, title, journal / booktitle, year, month, volume, number, pages,
%   doi, publisher, note
%
%   LaTeX is tidied up for the web page: "and" separates authors, "Last, First"
%   becomes "First Last", page ranges written -- become an en dash, and \& ~ \"o
%   and friends are cleaned up. `note` is shown in small text after the entry,
%   which is handy for "Best paper award".
%
% OPTIONAL EXTRA LINKS
%   An entry taken from ORCID shows a DOI link and nothing else, on purpose:
%   a clean one-line entry beats a row of links that all lead to the same page.
%   When you paste an entry here you can add any of these fields, and each one
%   becomes a link next to the DOI, in this order:
%
%     arxiv  = {2401.01234}             -> arXiv
%     pdf    = {https://.../paper.pdf}  -> pdf
%     slides = {https://.../talk.pdf}   -> slides
%     code   = {https://github.com/...} -> code
%     video  = {https://...}            -> video
%     url    = {https://...}            -> link
%
%   A full URL is used exactly as written; for `arxiv` you may give either the
%   full URL or just the identifier. These fields are NOT tidied up like the
%   others, because a URL may contain characters the tidy-up would rewrite.
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
